Item 02 is done. Every pebbling statement recorded in iteration 1 holds on every small input checked, with no counterexamples. The gate passes: `lake build` succeeds, the only `sorry` is the existing one (limit 1), and pytest shows 1251 passed, 2 skipped, 1 expected failure.

**What was built** (in `paper2/complex_check.py`):
- **Games:** four pebbling games, written from the sources' own wording. Each is decided exactly by searching every position that stays within a pebble budget, and each result is confirmed by showing one pebble fewer fails.
  - progressive black-white, under Lengauer's rules (turning a white pebble black is optional) and under Kirousis & Papadimitriou's (turning is automatic);
  - progressive black;
  - black-white and black with re-pebbling allowed.
- **Graph constructions:** Lengauer's two graphs built from a dag (`lengauer_u`, `lengauer_d`), the matrix whose MOSP value should equal the pebbling number (`pebble_matrix`), all acyclic orientations of a graph, and a chordality test.
- **How to run it:** `python -m paper2.complex_check --pebbling` takes 279 s on 32 cores and writes `paper2/data/pebbling_check.json`.

**What was checked:**
- every dag on up to 6 vertices, plus 20,000 random dags on 7;
- every small graph from the networkx atlas (at most 7 vertices) with vertices plus edges ≤ 14, for Lengauer's Theorem 3 (299 graphs);
- every graph on up to 7 vertices, over all its orientations (1,252 graphs);
- every rooted tree on up to 11 vertices, with arcs pointing both towards and away from the root (4,394);
- 359,744 play positions for the "no recontamination" lemma behind Kirousis & Papadimitriou's proof. It holds in every play that can still finish, not only in optimal ones.

**Findings:**
- **Lengauer's remark that re-pebbling doesn't help on trees fails earlier than we thought.** For trees directed away from the root, the smallest counterexample has 7 vertices, not 13 (Kirousis & Papadimitriou's ternary tree). It is a root with three legs of length 2: progressive pebbling needs 3 pebbles, with re-pebbling 2 suffice. No such tree on 6 or fewer vertices fails. Up to 11 vertices the gap is never more than 1.
- **On trees directed towards the root, re-pebbling never helped** in any of the 4,394 cases, in either game. That supports reading Lengauer's remark as being about those trees.
- **The gap between progressive and unrestricted pebbling grows.** On Lengauer's dag built from the complete graph Kₙ, progressive pebbling needs n + 1 pebbles for n = 2, 3, 4, while unrestricted pebbling needs 3 every time.

The results are written up as a new section P.7 at the end of `paper2/equivalences.md`, and earlier sections now point to it. The tests are in `tests/test_pebbling_check.py` (20 tests on hand-checked dags plus a quick full run). The only error found was one of my own hand-computed test values; the solver was right.

**Limits and what's left:**
- Dags are exhaustive only up to 6 vertices, and the node-search comparison covers graphs up to 6 vertices.
- I did not update `paper2/problem_transformations.md`; the new problem section and its proofs belong with Lean items 03–04.

